From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: allwinner: A733 eMMC on the real PMIC rails

The eMMC node was wired to two regulator-fixed placeholders (reg_vcc3v3 /
reg_vcc1v8). Those satisfy the binding and build green, but they are not the
rail: the driver cannot see or reason about the actual supply through them.
That is a known -110 (timeout) trap for exactly this node.

Wire the real ones instead:

  vmmc-supply  -> SWOUT2 (fed from DCDC1, 3.3 V), which carries VCC-eMMC
                  along with VCC-CARD, VCC-PH, VCC-IO and VCC33-USB-2
  vqmmc-supply -> CLDO5 (1.8 V), the eMMC VCCq, which also supplies VCC-PC -- the IO
                  rail of bank PC, i.e. the eMMC pins themselves

CLDO5 had no DT node yet (`arm64-dts-a733-enable-r-i2c0-axp318w-pmic` only declared swout1/swout2), so add it. The
regulator driver already supports it (`regulator-axp20x-add-support-for-the-AXP318W`: AXP_DESC_DO(AXP318, CLDO5, "cldo5",
..., AXP318_CLDO5_CONTROL) with control register 0x33).

Confirmed on silicon on the vendor stack driving this eMMC at
HS400: CLDO5 reg
0x33 = 0x0d = 1800 mV, DCDC1 reg 0x12 = 0x17 = 3300 mV, and the switch enables
reg 0x11 = 0x1f, i.e. both SWOUTs on. All three match the voltages above.

Neither node carries regulator-min/max-microvolt, and that is deliberate. A DT
window is enforcement, not a check: with both bounds present the kernel writes
the rail into the window at registration, which is how ELDO1 got pulled from
1.1 V to 0.95 V against trained, running DRAM. For cldo5 only a derived nominal
value is known, and a nominal value is not a documented floor, so no window
is written. The boot chain sets these
rails; Linux consumes them, and any deviation is for a userspace check to
report, which can only read and say so.

Leaving the window out does not break the eMMC's 1.8 V signalling. Without
regulator-min/max-microvolt the constraints keep min_uV == max_uV == 0, so
REGULATOR_CHANGE_VOLTAGE is never set (of_regulator.c:106). regulator_is_
supported_voltage() then takes its "if we can't change voltage check the current
voltage" path and compares the live rail against the requested range
(core.c:3492); mmc_regulator_set_vqmmc() asks for 1700000-1950000 for
MMC_SIGNAL_VOLTAGE_180, the rail sits at 1.8 V, and the helper returns 1 = "no
switch happened" rather than an error (regulator.c:156). Read, accept, do not
regulate -- which is the intent.

Both rails stay regulator-always-on. For SWOUT2 that is not cosmetic: it is a
shared switch feeding VCC-CARD, VCC-PH, VCC-IO and VCC33-USB-2 besides the
eMMC, so an mmc-core attempt to power the card down must not be able to take
half the board with it.

reg_vcc1v8 loses its last consumer here and is removed. reg_vcc3v3 stays: the
SD node (mmc0) still uses it, and moving SD to real rails is a separate change
-- bank PF is selectable between CLDO5 and SWOUT2 for UHS signalling, which
needs its own analysis and its own board test. SD works today and is not
touched here.

Not hardware-proven: this changes which regulator the eMMC node points at. The
rails themselves are already at these voltages from the boot chain, so no
voltage should move -- but that expectation is exactly what the next board test
has to confirm.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -524,14 +524,6 @@
 		regulator-always-on;
 	};
 
-	reg_vcc1v8: regulator-vcc1v8 {
-		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
-		regulator-name = "vcc1v8";
-		regulator-min-microvolt = <1800000>;
-		regulator-max-microvolt = <1800000>;
-		regulator-always-on;
-	};
-
 	/*
 	 * VCC5V0_USB20 -> the CH334F hub (USB-A J8 + on-board WiFi/BT), switched by
 	 * SGM2576 U10 whose enable is USB_HOST_EN = PM5 (schematic p7:172 / p13:92).
@@ -1014,8 +1006,8 @@
 			pinctrl-names = "default";
 			pinctrl-0 = <&mmc2_pins>, <&mmc2_clk_pins>,
 				    <&mmc2_ds_pins>;
-			vmmc-supply = <&reg_vcc3v3>;
-			vqmmc-supply = <&reg_vcc1v8>;
+			vmmc-supply = <&reg_swout2>;
+			vqmmc-supply = <&reg_cldo5>;
 			bus-width = <8>;
 			max-frequency = <200000000>;
 			non-removable;
@@ -1772,6 +1764,26 @@
 					 * that state until the net patches wire
 					 * swout1 as the gmac phy-supply.
 					 */
+					/*
+					 * eMMC VCCq and the IO supply of bank
+					 * PC, which carries the eMMC pins.
+					 * Deliberately carries NO
+					 * regulator-min/max-microvolt: a DT
+					 * window is enforcement, not a check
+					 * (the kernel writes the rail into it
+					 * at registration), and this rail has
+					 * no documented floor -- a nominal
+					 * value is not a floor, so none is
+					 * written.
+					 * Measured 1.8 V on the 6 GiB board and the 8 GiB board; the
+					 * boot chain sets it,
+					 * Linux only consumes it.
+					 */
+					reg_cldo5: cldo5 {
+						regulator-name = "vcc-pc";
+						regulator-always-on;
+					};
+
 					reg_swout1: swout1 {
 						regulator-name = "vcc33-phy";
 						regulator-always-on;
